"Princeton University Press Fall 2018 Catalog (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/F18Seasonal.pdf)\nciteturn654search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Left column is for “Adaptation and Natural Selection”; right column is for “The Calculus Gallery.” ... 92 Princeton Science Library ... WILLIAM DUNHAM ... Now with a new preface by the author, this book documents the evolution of calculus from a powerful but logically chaotic subject into one whose foundations are thorough, rigorous, and unflinching—a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, subtlest problems imaginable.\n\n“A beautifully written and excellently reasoned essay in defense of Darwinian selection as a sufficient theory to explain evolution without the necessity of group selection, population adaptation, or progress.”  \n—R. C. Lewontin, *Science*\n\n**George C. Williams** (1926–2010) was professor emeritus of ecology and evolution at the State University of New York at Stony Brook. **Richard Dawkins** is professor emeritus at the University of Oxford. An evolutionary biologist, he is the bestselling author of many books, including *The Selfish Gene, The God Delusion,* and *The Extended Phenotype*.\n\nNOVEMBER  \n978-0-691-18286-5  Paper  $22.95T  \n328 pages. 5½ × 8½.\n\nPOPULAR SCIENCE | BIOLOGY\n\n92 Princeton Science Library\n\nThe Calculus Gallery  \nMasterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\n\nWILLIAM DUNHAM  \nWith a new preface by the author\n\nMore than three centuries after its creation, calculus remains a dazzling intellectual achievement and the gateway to higher mathematics. This book charts its growth and development by sampling from the work of some of its foremost practitioners, beginning with Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in the late seventeenth century and continuing to Henri Lebesgue at the dawn of the twentieth. Now with a new preface by the author, this book documents the evolution of calculus from a powerful but logically chaotic subject into one whose foundations are thorough, rigorous, and unflinching—a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, subtlest problems imaginable. In touring *The Calculus Gallery*, we can see how it all came to be.\n\n“Mathematicians, scientists, and historians alike can learn much that is interesting, much that is mathematically significant, and a good deal that is both.”—Judith V. Grabiner, *Science*\n\n**William Dunham** is a Research Associate in Mathematics at Bryn Mawr College. He is the author of *Journey Through Genius: The Great Theorems of Mathematics, The Mathematical Universe,* and *Euler: The Master of Us All.*\n\nNOVEMBER  \n978-0-691-18285-8  Paper  $19.95T  \n256 pages. 63 b/w illus. 5½ × 8½.\n\nPOPULAR MATHEMATICS--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1287kvm)\nciteturn654search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Image: Cover of The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue ... WILLIAM DUNHAM ... Published by: Princeton University Press ... William Dunham lucidly presents the definitions, theorems, and proofs. ... Like a great museum,The Calculus Galleryis filled with masterpieces, among which are Bernoulli's early attack upon the harmonic series (1689), Euler's brilliant approximation of pi (1779), Cauchy's classic proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus (1823), Weierstrass's mind-boggling counterexample (1872), and Baire's original \"category theorem\" (1899).\n\nImage: Cover of The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\n\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\n\nWILLIAM DUNHAM\n\nCopyright Date: 2005\n\nPublished by: Princeton University Press\n\nPages: 256\n\nBook Info\n\nDescription\n\nMore than three centuries after its creation, calculus remains a dazzling intellectual achievement and the gateway into higher mathematics. This book charts its growth and development by sampling from the work of some of its foremost practitioners, beginning with Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in the late seventeenth century and continuing to Henri Lebesgue at the dawn of the twentieth--mathematicians whose achievements are comparable to those of Bach in music or Shakespeare in literature. William Dunham lucidly presents the definitions, theorems, and proofs. \"Students of literature read Shakespeare; students of music listen to Bach,\" he writes. But this tradition of studying the major works of the \"masters\" is, if not wholly absent, certainly uncommon in mathematics. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrinceton University Press Spring 2019 Catalog - Mathematics (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/math19.pdf)\nciteturn654search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; PRINCETON SCIENCE LIBRARY ... **The Calculus Gallery** ... Dunham charts its growth and development by sampling from the work of some of its foremost practitioners.Now with a new preface by the author, this book documents the evolution of calculus—a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, subtlest problems imaginable.\nPRINCETON SCIENCE LIBRARY\n\n“Clear and accessible…. Mathematicians, scientists, and historians alike can learn much that is interesting, much that is mathematically significant, and a good deal that is both.”—Judith V. Grabiner, *Science*\n\n**The Calculus Gallery**\n\nMore than three centuries after its creation, calculus remains a dazzling intellectual achievement and the gateway to higher mathematics. Dunham charts its growth and development by sampling from the work of some of its foremost practitioners. Now with a new preface by the author, this book documents the evolution of calculus—a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, subtlest problems imaginable.\n\n**WILLIAM DUNHAM** is a Research Associate in