vi). Princeton: Princeton University Press. https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400866793-fm\n\nDunham, W. 2005. Frontmatter. The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue. Princeton: Princeton University Press, pp. i-vi. https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400866793-fm\n\nDunham, William. \"Frontmatter\" In The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue, i-vi. Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2005. https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400866793-fm\n\n\n## Kapitel in diesem Buch\n\n  1. Frontmatter i\n  2. Contents vii\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews A. Bultheel (https://people.cs.kuleuven.be/~adhemar.bultheel/WWW/EMS/r260.php)\nciteturn654search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; title: The Calculus GalleryPublisher: Princeton University Press ... Dunham has chosen to tell the history of calculus from its origin, as conceived by Leibniz and Newton, till the moment that Lebesgue redefined Riemann's concept of an integral. ... The museum where his exhibition is displayed has twelve period rooms corresponding to as many chapters in the book, named after the artist-mathematicians who, if not produced, at least published the result(s).\nAuthor(s): William Dunham\ntitle: The Calculus Gallery\nPublisher: Princeton University Press\nYear: 2019\nISBN: 978-0-691-18285-8 (pbk)\nPages: 256 pp.\nPrice (tentative): £ 24.00 (net)\nURL: press.princeton.edu/titles/14169.html\nShort description:\nThis is a paperback reprint of the book originally published in 2005. It sketches the history of calculus from Newton and Leibniz till Lebesgue by a selection of key results during the evolution from a geometric/algebraic approach to a more abstract topological framework that was needed to cope with pathological cases when dealing with derivatives and integrals of functions. By restricting the discussion to functions of one real variable the book should be readable for students familiar with a basic calculus course.\nMSC main category: 00\nMSC category: 00A09\nOther MSC categories: 26-03, 01A05, 97A80  |  | \nImage: cover\nReview:\n\nThis is a slightly corrected reprint of the book originally published in 2005. The fact that it is now made available in the Princeton Science Library series as a cheaper version is a confirmation of its quality.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue - William Dunham - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=QnXSqvTiEjYC&lr=)\nciteturn654search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Image: Front Cover William Dunham Princeton University Press, 2005 - Mathematics - 236 pages More than three centuries after its creation, calculus remains a dazzling intellectual achievement and the gateway into higher mathematics. ... Like a great museum, The Calculus Gallery is filled with masterpieces, among which are Bernoulli's early attack upon the harmonic series (1689), Euler's brilliant approximation of pi (1779), Cauchy's classic proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus (1823), Weierstrass's mind-boggling counterexample (1872), and Baire's original \"category theorem\" (1899). ... Publisher  | Princeton University Press, 2005\nLike a great museum, The Calculus Gallery is filled with masterpieces, among which are Bernoulli's early attack upon the harmonic series (1689), Euler's brilliant approximation of pi (1779), Cauchy's classic proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus (1823), Weierstrass's mind-boggling counterexample (1872), and Baire's original \"category theorem\" (1899). Collectively, these selections document the evolution of calculus from a powerful but logically chaotic subject into one whose foundations are thorough, rigorous, and unflinching--a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, most subtle problems imaginable.\nAnyone who has studied and enjoyed calculus will discover in these pages the sheer excitement each mathematician must have felt when pushing into the unknown. In touring The Calculus Gallery, we can see how it all came to be.\n\n### About the author (2005)\n\nWilliam Dunham, Koehler Professor of Mathematics at Muhlenberg College, is the author of Journey Through Genius: The Great Theorems of Mathematics; The Mathematical Universe; and Euler: The Master of Us All. He has received the Mathematical Association of America's George Pólya, Trevor Evans, and Lester R. Ford awards, as well as its Beckenbach Prize for expository writing.\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\nAuthor  | William Dunham\nEdition  | illustrated, reprint\nPublisher  | Princeton University Press, 2005\nISBN  | 0691095655, 9780691095653\nLength  | 236 pages\nSubjects  | Mathematics › Calculus\nMathematics / Calculus\nMathematics / History & Philosophy\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirichlet function (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_function)\nciteturn654search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; - Dunham, William.The Calculus Gallery.Princeton University Press.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWilliam Dunham (mathematician) (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Dunham_%28mathematician%29)\nciteturn654search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Dunham won the American Association of Publishers' award for writing the Best Mathematics Book of 1994 for his book The Mathematical Universe. ... Princeton University Press. ... Review of The Calculus Gallery by William Dunham; and Musings of the Masters edited by Raymond G.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFerdinand von Lindemann (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_von_Lindemann)\nciteturn654search18 [wordl